#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162854} :activity/id #uuid "7a60cb12-dd77-5740-b1c2-9fd58434b948" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163229 :tutorial/content "Consider a quadratic form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. As we know, any symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized as follows:\n\n$$\nD = P^T \\! AP,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is an orthogonal matrix and $D$ is a diagonal matrix that contains eigenvalues of $A$ on the main diagonal. \n\nNow, substituting the change of variable $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}$ into our quadratic form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}\\,AP)y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,Dy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a canonical form. \n\nAs a result, using the orthogonal diagonalization of $A,$ we can find the canonical form of $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the canonical form of \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/43/43e0dc28161c0564a75f6ea2dc7f0e09ad74dffae05b864e13c61330221f2983.svg)\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/c9/c93cabd6a18df429b5aad99815b23e337c17b88a1c271dd17344631dfc5f8ebb.svg)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 - y_2^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "26652f7d-7ad4-54a0-b4a6-5d9faafa8403" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4888 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a819afd-fa45-5c12-8319-bbdf2452f54b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162892}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555"} :step/id #uuid "1325a77d-0093-5d23-9495-014e8f550773" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162893}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc"} :step/id #uuid "459f8fc0-d5b4-5716-9c7e-669c15d5ffdb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162894}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162894 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e"} :step/id #uuid "65c62c86-c42d-58f2-9237-353231d4f733"}] :activity/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form Using Orthogonal Transformations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162221}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049174 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162005 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161797 :question/id #uuid "ce37b130-8aa9-58af-b3b8-bf78b5a741e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8249" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+x_2^2+2x_3^2+4x_1x_2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/09/09d935cd479371e7cde4e52b2073cb23343a5137f311803c8da5879a59896fb4.svg)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/51/51694be12d594a39b6a07f48b37aedb47323fc5537a60f5d6a766576103d48f6.svg)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70838268-d60f-5cd4-9ed3-dccde36f454a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355658} {:answer/id #uuid "7be1e262-7df6-5cba-8c9b-119f464e0bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355754} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd07d02-7b67-5230-841c-4eb37efd16ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355934} {:answer/id #uuid "a7eea934-c642-5cf7-9185-b43061f811b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356152} {:answer/id #uuid "c514b938-dd01-5385-a0a0-885ebc665b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356386} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbe3819-de42-583d-8653-18ff814e0cc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357285}] :question/id #uuid "e7899e1b-4eee-518b-aa53-ed72b98bf89e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94446" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0254e5ff-cb6e-59c0-96cb-5313827be000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354677} {:answer/id #uuid "078eef9a-cb56-538b-97bd-463167fb3a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354730} {:answer/id #uuid "6adde8ba-a6ec-558e-bdca-221231ef50c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355610} {:answer/id #uuid "86480946-fd1f-5bce-bc6f-aab451882ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355851} {:answer/id #uuid "c216dfb6-6b14-550c-8211-39925d845f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355610} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b52084e-9dcf-57dd-84dc-8747c19747b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356961}] :question/id #uuid "72599bcc-35e4-5761-98ef-23a9eae51976" :question/math-academy-id "q-94449" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3345a2b6-259c-56c5-bf78-6c00b9e52a9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269737} {:answer/id #uuid "39dcc3da-5944-5514-8326-d8f61f9feb15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269797} {:answer/id #uuid "4704d3a0-8b95-5fee-9c8a-160e772b4d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269914} {:answer/id #uuid "791ac810-de05-595b-adcc-d614b417738a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270342} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b2901a-1802-5146-b269-1800aa065bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270342} :answer-field/id #uuid "bee350d2-bc95-5f36-a90e-914ce0d9336f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271857}] :question/id #uuid "27d94b0d-b387-5e7f-9e04-38b9668fec27" :question/math-academy-id "q-140987" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2413332d-82d9-5719-a72a-efe94e0bae11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358073} {:answer/id #uuid "378884b4-fd6c-53e2-ac2b-4beb14b32977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358242} {:answer/id #uuid "55eb775b-6742-5c39-b5f6-5237fa74d73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358498} {:answer/id #uuid "5962df92-19f8-57c3-9bca-80f52203ad52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358528} {:answer/id #uuid "8b66a136-e46c-5432-8325-b288ecd6048b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358498} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa25f90-1e65-598a-87af-9f51b5972d92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360382}] :question/id #uuid "82746228-1e72-5c4c-b5c5-f693d7e57c1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94898" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e992cdf-eecd-56ef-9371-efb9f91ba84a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358430} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6081e1-52e0-545a-990e-198f421f528f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358545} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca98af5-ffb5-531e-8877-5b50ef7a9e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358833} {:answer/id #uuid "8beb5025-3739-55e8-b562-061400cb72cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358966} {:answer/id #uuid "e1edc31b-6022-57ac-a872-baf4be9a8470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359748} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f141e3-1e65-51d7-ae95-87aaf36f6017" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360035}] :question/id #uuid "b7cf5716-597c-5d80-9235-85db49003378" :question/math-academy-id "q-94908" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c4e6bc-afd2-5630-97ec-773be2228c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550394} {:answer/id #uuid "901735db-1bf7-5c5f-b457-e88cba2e872b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550733} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdef02b-f2c0-5cf0-81a1-c3565e0d55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550780} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdc5c05-9bbe-541b-869c-9cf376ec10dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550882} {:answer/id #uuid "eda10016-5f60-5c0b-9d94-6cf80c8a71a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550882} :answer-field/id #uuid "9068f0e7-c225-543a-98c2-9fb66e6e943c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551153}] :question/id #uuid "e2384f15-0b1d-5ffb-9cdd-5f07e0af2003" :question/math-academy-id "q-140988" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n5{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d70dcb-3915-5dca-b77b-4d039c22fd0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}-5{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550459} {:answer/id #uuid "54be9190-6b23-5434-95db-5f490ba1a257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{y}_{1}^{2}+7{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550569} {:answer/id #uuid "63a7c54d-2bee-5bb4-a9cf-93c52af280d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550607} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe60f6c-f903-56ca-b937-a0a3cc617414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550883} {:answer/id #uuid "d18a843c-0b4d-5384-94d8-240379920e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{y}_{1}^{2}+7{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550569} :answer-field/id #uuid "47517d7d-e627-5322-bd0d-8ce492564569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551109}] :question/id #uuid "f4e82a18-a51f-5302-8e3f-b37a2f43e1e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94970" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,7,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n9{y}_{1}^{2}+7{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162006 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161798 :question/id #uuid "c8ec4ba0-e0bf-5842-8f5e-4914ce46706a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8250" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2,$ find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/69/69dcf821f3ff202b567b901d8f512c5cecf7159617ca51a0664c14a5099e9715.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/fc/fc9465d4e5bcd43a3c29e5946b05ec18cdbc7453a4011aa548f374016c561b45.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/59/59c1bd46325fab8a52fed162d7ae47e8fb33a10cc524cd770a3b18f5934e51dd.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/ad/adc9241219f3b5ebce0d8418bf34e250b7230ea612fabca5bdfc109c98a18dbb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/dc/dcab437eb77b29ab5bcd5c3ae14b77f10f01382aafadf2aecded1bb1fb72dc32.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/3f/3f6b1002893290dde6c9a68425b0035e249ff3630a41d29c9da5756c1a18f6a6.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c95d292b-b5d0-5097-a485-bcbedcb99071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161085} {:answer/id #uuid "cf435087-776e-5b47-9397-a41eb7fdb830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161086} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf02d81-6e1e-572d-a106-67f4f6532836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161087} {:answer/id #uuid "040f1313-e21c-514f-9c2e-75dfb47837fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161088} {:answer/id #uuid "39e03b5b-87da-5c0f-a60a-40325e38f947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161089}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ed668d09-a90d-5725-a6ce-1892307a482f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d40f64db-c375-5eb5-90f5-65b7daa42137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404708} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5211a6-75f4-56a1-852c-872334169c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404709} {:answer/id #uuid "2889008a-1c9c-54e0-a4b6-94388ce56e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404710} {:answer/id #uuid "8bacece9-6f5a-5672-9ede-4dd465de3062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404711} {:answer/id #uuid "1e966e11-c1da-51bf-87aa-b05920c029f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404708} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b952b55-79e1-55e1-ba57-d64596ae665f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404707}] :question/id #uuid "1587eada-a2ce-573f-9e77-1eddebc42115" :question/math-academy-id "q-94454" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230cd837-271b-59b8-8fc9-daa1826955f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550447} {:answer/id #uuid "367d39e1-bdbe-5145-9d88-c22d95aa558e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550501} {:answer/id #uuid "38a4a01f-1d6e-59d7-8eb3-95232ec04361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550502} {:answer/id #uuid "9bea0352-3bc6-5411-855c-6f3a0a33b11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550764} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ac0f69-989b-5238-8a66-cf117b0fbd78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551037} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfd39ae2-8344-5a25-a125-e32c39919730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161210}] :question/id #uuid "14118443-b386-51d2-bcd8-a6f6bd250385" :question/math-academy-id "q-95064" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =10,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12bf9c48-adf5-5505-9acf-470bcd00e2e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269433} {:answer/id #uuid "28ba23a7-0de5-509b-82c4-df9110368ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269634} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5b8660-6902-5581-9ba0-3793ddcec3a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269675} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c8a43e-5e4e-5fb1-9edb-1b9646ab1963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271301} {:answer/id #uuid "efaafb17-4207-5a9d-9693-07fa68e3b409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271301} :answer-field/id #uuid "787c368e-889d-5b8c-b239-21ca9558eae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271724}] :question/id #uuid "6b8238cd-632f-5ae3-b721-832a7f9669c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140998" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =10,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{1}0}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178f1a53-b088-503c-9e1c-e483af83c450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357956} {:answer/id #uuid "397463a5-5679-57da-9a4f-269e65a0067b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358258} {:answer/id #uuid "63801535-5db3-5fa4-adf2-a0a612b71714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358617} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9887c3-7818-533a-a7be-08484715473d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359004} {:answer/id #uuid "e74b23e9-0a62-589c-8a56-1890f0262afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358617} :answer-field/id #uuid "71057f21-bf84-51ad-b440-b6a3ef2a0bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360182}] :question/id #uuid "574f8096-6cd3-5243-b06d-c1422dfabc1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95071" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -40 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-8)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d23a1d-c5cb-5263-97ae-c1d2aee936d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -15\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358186} {:answer/id #uuid "58cc887c-1f90-584f-b269-2479e33dc2d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358522} {:answer/id #uuid "831b1872-6d21-50bd-badf-8c555a95dd41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358904} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d95938-4e3e-5324-958a-18c961de80a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359165} {:answer/id #uuid "f720c207-dde0-55ae-835a-5c47400c2515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -15\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358522} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f052b1-64d0-5f2d-bf08-da51612219c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360342}] :question/id #uuid "85c2527c-a204-5bec-a809-539cc736fb33" :question/math-academy-id "q-95092" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}14-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-20\\lambda +75 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =15,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-15\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669f4399-98d0-58cb-b5ca-020a4654ee8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364546} {:answer/id #uuid "43a786aa-dee4-53e1-9bc6-44e46ac40c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364547} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f840fe-347c-55e1-bbf6-32e391995c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364548} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b0a414-8efb-5f7c-aa2d-597889961eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364549} {:answer/id #uuid "6efe3c1a-3df7-5ca6-9447-5ad51ed877d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364550} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f96ff58-af7b-58d2-b1bc-1f8c4ba99615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364545}] :question/id #uuid "a7869b9c-6fbb-54b1-92e1-8d96639ffbe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95065" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b279401-b2e9-52e3-8e8a-5ed7f4ab0fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400904} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9a7c28-297a-59f1-8324-969697fcb145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400905} {:answer/id #uuid "6872e2bc-f5d0-5fd1-8dae-d3fd4d09b23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400906} {:answer/id #uuid "432384f9-da06-525e-baa9-7350f4251ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400907} {:answer/id #uuid "67ba60db-8cbf-553b-b63c-195669a23aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400906} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d3fb41-4434-5c99-a083-41daf1b7f0a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400903}] :question/id #uuid "2172cdc2-fb35-5393-84ba-3c3dbc3c7926" :question/math-academy-id "q-140997" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22954aba-c66f-5f85-9e45-9aa6ce4d72bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404768} {:answer/id #uuid "f4383665-6278-580b-8943-01438ebff475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404769} {:answer/id #uuid "58794a90-ab7c-51d4-af96-b3bf9bf1f667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404770} {:answer/id #uuid "19eaeeb0-70cc-5efc-903b-969f1a50d985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404771} {:answer/id #uuid "42e7fff7-d8da-538d-9740-2d40d6c93c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404769} :answer-field/id #uuid "af751c8a-d34c-5ece-8db8-ee7406d5299f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404767}] :question/id #uuid "0d232faf-2de0-5a39-91fc-eea4fa2a2bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95069" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162007 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161799 :question/id #uuid "243d1a75-6f0f-5d55-a7ec-333487fa37bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8251" :question/problem "![](images/61/6135fe578745ebf2eccb4370557f0dba6ec27361a192e4e87536eadad87a39bf.png)\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=4, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=2, \\quad$ and $\\quad\\lambda_3=1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/13/13eee082d48c56e6c7e796549eddc0185868b39841a7c5946ce9ce9e996ba553.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/1e/1e7c81e373377bd719e1bbc9e87a8c88d13f6116bcf2949897fc26246e2265d1.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/61/61311029da90b54253a552ce2198ce9193d7f343c3ab345eea25419444da00a3.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/3b/3bf5334469b8b135548dfbd0c35725ee6927e2a169c6db37a1b0bae3f62695e5.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/1c/1cb1b34b0df191e9fa63a907ba59e26a2ad16f3977e97d5b8588dc5e51f2c3a0.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3  \\| = 1,$ we get \n![](images/39/39bf82ed6be834f41140a70e048268b39d47d7a0944461247d312d4efe95d184.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/fb/fb5604c6dc892a34af523d6b9b769cd50c59e82ed65d7be47cb89ed6044125bf.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93afd1e7-589c-572d-81e8-cd95c2c7e1a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161091} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ead3be-52cb-51bc-a4ec-b140d0258480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161092} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5ddbfb-38cf-5614-b107-bae7c3df3a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161093} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce52d2a-928e-513a-97af-4fe0bcce819b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161094} {:answer/id #uuid "5809b31c-60e6-5689-a138-5e6ee5628d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161095}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a39de5df-822b-5722-89ab-814c839cfed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161090} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a042fc8f-539f-5bdb-bddf-c5fe30041a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394978} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc77117-300e-5b56-8c2f-140816b1741a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394979} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8d7eed-8c68-5288-bf38-d8900425c601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394980} {:answer/id #uuid "e217cdf1-3dc8-57d9-9626-db83e92641ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394981} {:answer/id #uuid "97cc3ca7-68d4-575c-b995-c7ff1f31637d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394981} :answer-field/id #uuid "d94e720f-93b6-56f5-a9cd-79db4928f0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394977}] :question/id #uuid "7f0148f4-83df-5e99-8325-e264016c452b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94461" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc8877a5-3ad2-52bb-a064-0496ec64af0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161217} {:answer/id #uuid "de86953f-2dc6-5b88-b2b6-6650bf35709