inally, since ${l}_{32}=2$ and ${u}_{33}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4920e6-9ee2-5600-ac93-e931db1e2977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557891} {:answer/id #uuid "38eb9b08-b5bf-5368-b8d9-361707b6a46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557982} {:answer/id #uuid "5746845c-7dff-5056-8896-7d3ad03d69e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558031} {:answer/id #uuid "5a131042-b0f8-570b-b699-fbba493ec068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558037} {:answer/id #uuid "db457332-571d-5aeb-a7ed-3cef5e2f2957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558037} :answer-field/id #uuid "de84d423-224e-5c74-abae-3facb0dd0501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558465}] :question/id #uuid "4e8f6f22-5a79-521e-9e1b-64d5b0f419c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-106973" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 14 & -16 \\\\ 3 & 13 & -11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 14 & -16 \\\\ 3 & 13 & -11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ \\,\\,4\\,\\, & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 13 & -11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ \\,\\,4\\,\\, & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ \\,\\,4\\,\\, & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & \\,2\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=2$ and ${u}_{23}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{23}=2\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0211b914-a3ae-5d39-96e7-5b2cb42a8917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557872} {:answer/id #uuid "0926bad3-ceea-5bce-aa7e-a06c7496873b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557888} {:answer/id #uuid "5407c4b5-0e28-519e-871d-43c3446c346e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558023} {:answer/id #uuid "642d6672-abac-56a6-96f1-b0dc1f85b7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558061} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbeace3-e761-54c4-ae18-6b154b6e0618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558061} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9300cd1-5fe4-5912-9d79-a9aa7d5af107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558462}] :question/id #uuid "afd80dcb-1213-5376-8192-e6d4df545870" :question/math-academy-id "q-32645" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ \\,\\,0\\,\\, & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{31}=0$ and ${u}_{23}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=0\\cdot 1=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39302ced-d4eb-597f-93d1-372de3bfb010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557983} {:answer/id #uuid "442fb9de-8df6-5b2e-b613-6a42f276ef2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558002} {:answer/id #uuid "b148bbb5-8d84-5061-ab0b-7c46b63fdc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558218} {:answer/id #uuid "e737d6e2-ff52-504a-9ff2-2a4d9eb9526b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558328} {:answer/id #uuid "e978ccd8-1135-596e-9c57-c3d779a0e901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558328} :answer-field/id #uuid "592fdd22-e6c1-5811-8eda-6492d03016e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558400}] :question/id #uuid "d3f95eef-078c-51a3-be11-2d73ad369bc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105889" :question/problem "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $C=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $C$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $C$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 3 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 3 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{21}=1$ and ${u}_{23}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=1\\cdot 3=3.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1781 :topic/title "LU Factorization of NxN Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176729} :activity/id #uuid "eaad5957-8a77-5c3e-bc8d-81b1c2325346" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e"} :step/id #uuid "78c5549c-e0d5-53d1-a816-9e9092586d36"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176994 :tutorial/content "For some $n\\times n$ matrices, it's possible to construct a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting entirely of linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = 5, \\lambda_2 = 4,$ and $\\lambda_3 = 2,$ with corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors \n\n![](images/45/451c66729d04278ec42f1ffcc558245b5cd2ef14b0c3d14dbd35447702b7b873.svg)\nThese eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nHowever, there exist $n\\times n$ matrices whose eigenvectors do *not* form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n![](images/65/653166c6712102e8afacb5e2a73de617a3777e6812d9abbeb760f72b664525b3.svg)\n\nhas the single eigenvalue ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ However, there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, namely\n\n![](images/50/5007b5e49662ccb6aad508b233f4d107bdb89afc2607eee76280a16ec02d6f90.svg)\n\nSince there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we say that the **geometric multiplicity** of ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ equals  $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "931f2f11-a4be-5b57-83cc-47ca54fc5443" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5420 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3961ed3f-bdbb-58d8-946c-67fb1150da8f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4"} :step/id #uuid "fb412f74-1e1a-5c76-a91c-35d89b5cd5f4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176731}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176995 :tutorial/content "Recall that a generalized eigenvector of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of  $A$ is a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ In other words, $A-\\lambda I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $n$ steps. This is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)^n \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n(A - \\lambda I)^{n-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBy the definition of the null space of a matrix, $(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v} \\in \\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^k.$\nThis, in turn, means that a generalized eigenvector of rank $n$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^n$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^{n-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8b/8b0e00c74440c269a1d1b1c076aad56dfe3a056a1493c28862e4d080b5c33563.png)\n\nFor example, suppose that a matrix $A$ has the characteristic polynomial \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (5-\\lambda)^6(\\lambda-3)^2(\\lambda+4)^5.\n$$\n\nIn addition, let's assume the following:\n\n- The nullity of $(A+4I)^3$ equals $2.$\n\n- The nullity of $(A+4I)^4$ equals $3.$\n\nIf a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -4?$\n\nFor the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = -4,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A + 4I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n(A + 4I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(A +4I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(A +4I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A+4I)^3 = 3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4.$\n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is equal to \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\lambda I)^n - \\text{nullity}(A-\\lambda I)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3504196-e96f-544f-89f9-a985b82f8ddb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5421 :tutorial/title "The Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank for a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "cbffc464-3ec8-51a7-bad6-e40d2c96d1fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05"} :step/id #uuid "32f06230-5f13-50e6-adb6-a7143bdba331" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177052 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has the characteristic polynomial \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^4 (1-\\lambda)^3(1+\\lambda)^7,\n$$\n\nand that there are precisely $4$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = -1.$ \n\nIf a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -1?$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- In the characteristic polynomial, the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $7.$ So, the *algebraic* multiplicity of $\\lambda = -1$ is $7.$\n\n- However, we're told that there are only $4$ eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = -1.$ So, the *geometric* multiplicity of $\\lambda = -1$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = -1$ is \n\n$$\n7-4=3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ccaaceee-ceed-5a3f-be84-18703a3e27a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6126 :tutorial/title "A Second Example"} :step/id #uuid "b8ab2409-9530-5042-8db2-6e6b344401ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177053 :tutorial/content "So, not every $n\\times n$ matrix has the property that its eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$\n\nDespite this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*For any real $n \\times n$ matrix, there is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting of generalized eigenvectors of the matrix.*\n\nTo illustrate, let's go back to our matrix \n![](images/d0/d09805a20bbc23954fdec5a893e05ec0bad45a6503e27354df60b4d71952f990.svg)\n\nwhere the eigenvectors corresponding to the single eigenvalue $\\lambda = 4$ are\n\n![](images/e6/e6053d03e5e0f4bc843856d88dbc9e8e14252200a5d7280a4a05872b19bd4d56.svg)\n\nRemember that $\\lambda = 4$ has *algebraic* multiplicity $3$ and *geometric* multiplicity $2.$\n\nNow, it's easy to show that $A$ has a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ given by\n\n![](images/89/890d3b69aa88f0f442760d03afc7333d2e918b5b3401a33b167cfa40321a5f4c.svg)\nand our generalized eigenvectors form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, ${\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{u}}}, {\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{v}}},$ and $\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$ form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ In particular, our basis consists of\n\n- two eigenvectors ($\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$), and\n\n- one generalized eigenvector whose rank is greater than one ($\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$).\n\nNotice that the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{algebraic multiplicity}-\\text{geometric multiplicity} & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is no coincidence. In general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "ad92ba79-e1b7-5818-9d69-6fd643580cdb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6127 :tutorial/title "Forming a Basis Using Generalized Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "5899fe18-80e2-5145-909a-f3f95dbb69db" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176835}}] :activity/title "Ranks of Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176150} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501245} :activity/id #uuid "c193cfcf-aa19-53c0-9e94-a4fca834c89a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364434 :question/id #uuid "d180b2be-eb75-50b7-9052-2821851f23f8"} :step/id #uuid "08414635-bff3-5a7f-b66e-3fbe87007867" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176232 :question/id #uuid "b2323904-dbe3-5d6e-b9ad-cd7ee2cf8fc9"} :step/id #uuid "8dc09aaa-a187-577a-a919-a2d9e3f5ef8f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501145 :question/id #uuid "18821d40-33b7-5a84-97bc-878b35f96c25"} :step/id #uuid "96d9823d-b5f4-5ae5-9b3d-4022dff3b3dd"}] :activity/title "Ranks of Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500467}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049190 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175795 :question/id #uuid "13cf1317-c3a1-5175-9f5b-c3da13cad348" :question/math-academy-id "e-6744" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (\\lambda-4)^6(\\lambda-9)^8,$ the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ and the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14 \\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 14-9 = 5.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ of dimension $5$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 = 14-7 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ of dimension $7$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $5$ in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 7-5=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=9.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519aff8a-0f6a-5132-8a28-63205c2a9b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362799} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2c9519-f70f-51b9-82ab-52d0dbaf22ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362800} {:answer/id #uuid "c7048870-5f85-522e-bdaa-af8db4e61edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362801} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1c84a6-d5a8-554b-9862-b205e66c5f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362802} {:answer/id #uuid "b4930134-8b4b-5956-805b-10a2435ebb5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362801} :answer-field/id #uuid "78419c12-db9b-50fa-ad3a-2e955bd295b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362798}] :question/id #uuid "11749f57-f853-5eba-906e-45841d04e575" :question/math-academy-id "q-109922" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +\\sqrt{2})^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{21},$ the rank of $(B-2I)^{6}$ equals $4,$ and the rank of $(B-2I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{24}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-2I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-2I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $B$ must be a $24\\times 24$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(B-2I)^{5}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{5}=24-7=17.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B-2I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}$ of dimension $17$ in ${R}^{24}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(B-2I)^{6}$ equals $4,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{6}=24-4=20.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B-2I)^{6}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$ of dimension $20$ in ${R}^{24}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}$ is a subspace of dimension $17$ in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$, which is a subspace of dimension $20,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{6}-\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{5}=20-17=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94209dd4-8332-5a59-a665-1704486cfa93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175553} {:answer/id #uuid "a5527931-a3b1-55fa-9b63-7717e8e64f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175554} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa7a214-ba76-554e-beac-ba26ce0b267b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175555} {:answer/id #uuid "556528e8-e14c-5170-a694-d9d52bcbb8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175556} {:answer/id #uuid "7cda7705-15f5-55b4-9121-da930449a99b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175557} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a6c438-eab2-5a25-b355-d190884b3e3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175552}] :question/id #uuid "e04a0ca0-a400-5c37-86e4-06c47bca3608" :question/math-academy-id "q-78160" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{4},$ the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ and the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6.$ If a basis of ${R}^{8}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be an $8\\times 8$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=8-7=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ of dimension $1$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}=8-6=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ of dimension $2$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e11286c-a986-57fe-8281-0e5ec526b7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362793} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3eacb4-6fbb-51f1-b864-a9342ca4bf61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362794} {:answer/id #uuid "27d81a69-2275-5b2d-9558-6554485b6749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362795} {:answer/id #uuid "83afea75-2dba-57b4-b57e-5f5c24090657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362796} {:answer/id #uuid "40a15b2b-0eb1-5f56-84d3-0d74547eaa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362794} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2afe33e-7b75-520b-b1f3-f59bd3ce321b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362792}] :question/id #uuid "33dd51f4-4d1c-5237-bfae-c27c6505f314" :question/math-academy-id "q-109921" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(2-\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda +\\pi )^{15}(\\lambda -1)^{4},$ the rank of $(B+\\pi I)^{4}$ equals $8,$ and the rank of $(B+\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $10.$ If a basis of ${R}^{22}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\pi ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B+\\pi I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B+\\pi I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $B$ must be a $22\\times 22$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(B+\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $10,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+\\pi I)^{3}=22-10=12.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B+\\pi I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{3}$ of dimension $12$ in ${R}^{22}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(B+\\pi I)^{4}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+\\pi I)^{4}=22-8=14.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B+\\pi I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{4}$ of dimension $14$ in ${R}^{22}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $12$ in $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $14,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{4}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+\\pi I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(B+\\pi I)^{3}=14-12=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e3c901c-7312-50f9-84f3-0847c6c873c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362806} {:answer/id #uuid "4af5f7b9-d947-5e23-b64d-073e7806e8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362807} {:answer/id #uuid "23bea6c5-368f-54f5-bd60-72fcea30b8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362808} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a40cd6-0487-5966-8107-fc8de5ad2677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362809} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3eebf5-41f1-53ad-94a2-86f371ec406d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362808} :answer-field/id #uuid "b81cda8c-6bf7-5282-9767-416c2a0ce085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362805}] :question/id #uuid "4ee15711-0318-50bd-a684-4cae61e75cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-109923" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda -\\sqrt{3})^{18}(\\lambda +3)^{7},$ the rank of $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$ equals $8,$ and the rank of $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}$ equals $10.$ If a basis of ${R}^{25}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $C$ must be a $25\\times 25$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}$ equals $10,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}=25-10=15.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}$ of dimension $15$ in ${R}^{25}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}=25-8=17.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$ of dimension $17$ in ${R}^{25}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}$ is a subspace of dimension $15$ in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$, which is a subspace of dimension $17,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}-\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}=17-15=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5a6652-a5b2-5191-9452-42adbad19a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364429} {:answer/id #uuid "a48e73bc-9519-5d1b-8cc8-f307eb10cb49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364430} {:answer/id #uuid "d70e8768-3188-5a71-af3a-588d4baf5c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364431} {:answer/id #uuid "813178d1-1b6f-58f1-b783-cf2b0730e9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364432} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab2bf39-e721-5ed2-ae49-e2712e1b5fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364430} :answer-field/id #uuid "29bd9632-6f50-5c1f-b387-83d14f2c12e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364428}] :question/id #uuid "64ce957b-4cc9-5f51-89ac-72c2d0fd01aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-78493" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda -8)^{4}(\\lambda +4)^{11},$ the rank of $(A+4I)^{5}$ equals $6,$ and the rank of $(A+4I)^{4}$ equals $8.$ If a basis of ${R}^{15}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+4I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $15\\times 15$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A+4I)^{4}$ equals $8,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^{4}=15-8=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A+4I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{4}$ of dimension $7$ in ${R}^{15}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A+4I)^{5}$ equals $6,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^{5}=15-6=9.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A+4I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{5}$ of dimension $9$ in ${R}^{15}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $7$ in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $9,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{5}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^{4}=9-7=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df20e00-8b95-5d9c-9ad7-b28badfc60f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364436} {:answer/id #uuid "95cab23e-3490-5b26-810e-f8f4b6bb69ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364437} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebef18c-06dd-5776-88b8-f04701e33f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364438} {:answer/id #uuid "25f77071-51f5-53cc-b0f7-f53de32e9e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364439} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee16813-be34-5924-a753-6535fdc0bd29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364436} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d5de73d-f30c-59bc-9085-0f3566bc1afd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364435}] :question/id #uuid "d180b2be-eb75-50b7-9052-2821851f23f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-78494" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5)^{19},$ the rank of $(A-5I)^{5}$ equals $12,$ and the rank of $(A-5I)^{6}$ equals $9.$ If a basis of ${R}^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $20\\times 20$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-5I)^{5}$ equals $12,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{5}=20-12=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-5I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{5}$ of dimension $8$ in ${R}^{20}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-5I)^{6}$ equals $9,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{6}=20-9=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-5I)^{6}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$ of dimension $11$ in ${R}^{20}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{5}$ is a subspace of dimension $8$ in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$, which is a subspace of dimension $11,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{6}-\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{5}=11-8=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d77cf98f-dc2d-597e-a58a-381d47b24fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375736} {:answer/id #uuid "83ff8887-2c73-56fe-b953-d516e5ec148d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375737} {:answer/id #uuid "119ebc3c-0a43-527e-ab2a-fdbbe9b359a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375738} {:answer/id #uuid "28a870e1-ca22-5b80-b2f8-b171f79975c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375739} {:answer/id #uuid "25f46f11-2afc-5ea5-9fcd-6e2d49537353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375737} :answer-field/id #uuid "590ba047-135b-5692-b98d-d34851d9e5a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375735}] :question/id #uuid "c8f2e0c6-0647-57c4-a136-1d5064af2223" :question/math-academy-id "q-109926" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda -3)^{8}(\\lambda +12)^{6},$ the rank of $(D-3I)^{4}$ equals $9,$ and the rank of $(D-3I)^{5}$ equals $8.$ If a basis of ${R}^{16}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D-3I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D-3I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $16\\times 16$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-3I)^{4}$ equals $9,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-3I)^{4}=16-9=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-3I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-3I)^{4}$ of dimension $7$ in ${R}^{16}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-3I)^{5}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-3I)^{5}=16-8=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-3I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-3I)^{5}$ of dimension $8$ in ${R}^{16}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(D-3I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $7$ in $\\text{Null}(D-3I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $8,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(D-3I)^{5}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D-3I)^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-3I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(D-3I)^{4}=8-7=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2a744a-7e8d-5a8d-8a93-dc76818c873e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490527} {:answer/id #uuid "13a5a88c-0b5a-5a89-b737-dc9f96f87647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490550} {:answer/id #uuid "6a001863-7677-56f5-a48e-46de61c50db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490712} {:answer/id #uuid "76c17061-49ab-5782-90a2-1a974e55e8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490746} {:answer/id #uuid "f5678664-def6-56da-8fb7-91cc385210a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490981} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f04c93-2db7-56ff-bf49-28c30a11d6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491079}] :question/id #uuid "247f09ea-5442-5e08-b3bd-d68a92ad4690" :question/math-academy-id "q-78478" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -7)^{12},$ the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$ and the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=14-5=9.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ of dimension $9$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}=14-3=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ of dimension $11$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $9$ in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $11,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=11-9=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169df782-f22f-53ee-888a-ef5dd9042bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559066} {:answer/id #uuid "2155da94-081e-506a-911b-03855a5f2cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559096} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e04b3f-f522-5411-be37-966761ea2f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559524} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcd5536-0adc-5f39-92ab-7c7a8048c753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559557} {:answer/id #uuid "e8356a21-b88d-5892-a5fb-1e440a90d6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559066} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4be26dd-2c06-59f0-a37a-9169047bccde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559764}] :question/id #uuid "e1e17bba-2392-58cc-bb67-1a8adce57d23" :question/math-academy-id "q-109927" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda -7)^{3}(\\lambda +4)^{2}(\\lambda -9)^{9},$ the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ and the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D-9I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D-9I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=14-11=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ of dimension $3$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}=14-8=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ of dimension $6$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=6-3=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175835 :question/id #uuid "faa37d23-bdd2-5651-bf53-47835ee61cc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9055" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5,$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1,$ there are exactly $3$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5,\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda = 1$ has multiplicity $5.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $5.$\n\nHowever, there are only $3$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 1$ is $5-3=2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bab197-73af-5629-bd73-9a2539823707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173741} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1ab9c9-85f4-531d-b3a7-bea26e2d3c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173742} {:answer/id #uuid "3c42c8d8-a86c-5a0e-9310-317060bb58bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173743} {:answer/id #uuid "5029f1b5-3ff3-57ee-bad8-a1511f8c44c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173744} {:answer/id #uuid "69acfa87-eb10-5b78-aa51-34879b469042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173742} :answer-field/id #uuid "575ab359-7284-535c-aa54-794bcba6804d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173740}] :question/id #uuid "3cb467c0-ea77-596f-b372-4352d634576f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109740" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $3-2=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84e7677b-e65b-5ba2-88a8-2bd2acc82160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175541} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbab60-450c-5620-a2e5-32645332926c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175542} {:answer/id #uuid "0990d12f-cfc1-56df-8402-1297de8e7a0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175543} {:answer/id #uuid "10c7f86f-3c81-5a11-bcd4-52e71a004a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175544} {:answer/id #uuid "577a436a-e117-5f95-9bc1-fba890d25d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175544} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa3a7e0-2f97-5889-a2d5-5385c643040a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175540}] :question/id #uuid "07ef05dc-0a2c-5317-9865-4e3852ecab01" :question/math-academy-id "q-78129" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(3-\\lambda )^{4}(5+\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(3-\\lambda )^{4}(5+\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-5$ has multiplicity $6.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ is $6.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-5$ is $6-2=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e23481a5-a245-58f5-9277-aa451e3cd5e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362691} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e228ad-32ee-5207-8f11-ace4458ad975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362692} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5bedc9-906b-5fdf-afbc-28280706a045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362693} {:answer/id #uuid "d092f86e-9313-538f-8643-4caea5dfbfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362694} {:answer/id #uuid "58e83042-c2b9-530f-b764-75ead7c985da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362691} :answer-field/id #uuid "22f2c0f2-4235-55fe-bb2a-fe0145419085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362690}] :question/id #uuid "a9b4e2b6-fe2e-5e76-8aaf-86f9732030f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109667" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(4+\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)^{4}(\\lambda +2)^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ there are exactly $4$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(4+\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)^{4}(\\lambda +2)^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-2$ has multiplicity $7.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ is $7.$\n\nHowever, there are only $4$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2$ is $7-4=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f68615-eb24-57c3-9487-d95f29548b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362698} {:answer/id #uuid "82504c4d-6f8d-5369-81b8-3c96a6333b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362699} {:answer/id #uuid "f581bada-6705-5dc0-b88f-a34adb8369b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362700} {:answer/id #uuid "328a29ee-aa7f-53df-8311-2051c4991c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362701} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d2eeec-d5b8-5dfc-b26b-494883e9b7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362698} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc0ea033-e8d5-588f-89a8-fc8d90f8bdee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362697}] :question/id #uuid "52518e4f-46f0-5ab2-8f1e-9e00bf15aaf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109745" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(8-\\lambda )^{6}(4+\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda -1)^{5},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$ there are exactly $4$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(8-\\lambda )^{6}(4+\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda -1)^{5},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =8$ has multiplicity $6.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ is $6.$\n\nHowever, there are only $4$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =8$ is $6-4=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e865e05-d063-5259-9427-8e9ac3415420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d54eb7-8c94-5ba1-8ff1-0e33e8e8ff4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364423} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca18b90-c676-50ef-bb00-24c4e41c4b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364424} {:answer/id #uuid "61161b93-ea6b-5325-aa7b-d6e1119b0a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364425} {:answer/id #uuid "1c60cb57-ff4f-5029-8ce2-b8fe29a39c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b1e17b-e37b-5c86-92c1-41321a65eb42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364421}] :question/id #uuid "246e62ce-be4d-58bc-8aa3-d8a9e5b8b661" :question/math-academy-id "q-78463" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $4-1=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195c4aec-4206-524d-999f-7c4101d2d7fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386447} {:answer/id #uuid "ba293136-d659-561c-8537-09a5722e5b25" :answer/typ