\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $3-2=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "382f6341-07e9-5d75-adb4-5a4fcdfe016a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386454} {:answer/id #uuid "a98c0a47-fad4-54bd-9a08-1016c8c5e268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386455} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e5851d-1f19-524f-9845-a03add07f035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386456} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4a0c0b-1762-5e1b-a78b-303307949962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386457} {:answer/id #uuid "235fcb2d-aad6-533c-8d07-75611f87ebf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386454} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f5269a-bb75-592f-9f58-18875884cd1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386453}] :question/id #uuid "4a37675f-e872-5fca-bf67-03df4f2e5cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-109741" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $S$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(e-\\lambda )^{5}(\\lambda +\\sqrt{3})^{4}(5-\\lambda )^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{16}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $S,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(e-\\lambda )^{5}(\\lambda +\\sqrt{3})^{4}(5-\\lambda )^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =e$ has multiplicity $5.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e$ is $5.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =e$ is $5-2=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d41baa7-2f99-5aa9-9e20-9c3e4ee48d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490534} {:answer/id #uuid "7112d1d7-2482-5dbe-9b34-f2f88cda3ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490733} {:answer/id #uuid "a1eb0cde-a682-5537-836e-88895a46e126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490842} {:answer/id #uuid "b461c956-eb03-540c-937a-5468ab8906b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490870} {:answer/id #uuid "d859ee8e-2941-509f-9843-675fc04e101f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490842} :answer-field/id #uuid "13419c69-6e14-595f-b394-69e8b9ff2c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491010}] :question/id #uuid "ccaaf964-e8e6-5357-a7a2-fd338a200e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-109743" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $4-1=3.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175844 :question/id #uuid "bed5f45f-5c76-542b-965f-eadf474823c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9125" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (3+\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-\\pi)^8(\\lambda-2)^3,$ the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^3$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^4$ equals $6.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ has dimension $6.$ This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $6.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^3 = 6-4=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=\\pi.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9915c4f7-fec8-5821-a1e5-91cb9f24bded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375715} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb84d03-63e5-5ac4-bfb3-a8e737d4a931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375716} {:answer/id #uuid "0d440d47-6467-5c2e-935a-ea22542facfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375717} {:answer/id #uuid "c1183feb-ea61-579f-895e-c9fbfacb3ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375718} {:answer/id #uuid "d954d438-acb2-5e8f-94fb-1b1426a9576e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375717} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce26ad16-b594-507c-bab8-79b00e68ef59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375714}] :question/id #uuid "b2323904-dbe3-5d6e-b9ad-cd7ee2cf8fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109121" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -5)^{7},$ the nullity of $(A-5I)^{2}$ equals $1,$ and the nullity of $(A-5I)^{3}$ equals $2.$ If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}$ has dimension $1.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $1.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f99e9009-e6c2-582d-90ae-d0bfcf1f5d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362725} {:answer/id #uuid "99d135b5-1334-5d73-b629-c04ae8d41c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362726} {:answer/id #uuid "2dac6776-db44-5e53-8d0e-7467411d2060" :answer/type {:db/id