r/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500580} {:answer/id #uuid "58f930ba-330b-56ca-943a-67237cb474f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500648} {:answer/id #uuid "84bfa36c-7054-5b53-81f3-b9e1906d6186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500742} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c4aa88-43ec-5d07-ae83-df734fe4814c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500648} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7e5239-25ea-5909-b9ce-6ad812ed18c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501131}] :question/id #uuid "18821d40-33b7-5a84-97bc-878b35f96c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-109747" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{5}(\\lambda +3)^{2},$ the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $3.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-\\pi I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{2}=3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14be5dd6-fd36-5a08-a677-f6f5e4980f1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564971} {:answer/id #uuid "5e88b7b2-c4a2-5f46-b0d0-299f26aaf066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565082} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ded33d-31c7-56d3-a266-03c82ae475fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565217} {:answer/id #uuid "de2afa21-f6ff-5915-a8ab-239729b5c4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565269} {:answer/id #uuid "e72807a2-0780-5981-82b2-b6a4a2adee11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564971} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1973d3-4cfd-50f8-81d5-f1c3b35c4846" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565359}] :question/id #uuid "fe770e3a-7d81-54be-9346-77bf6627bbf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109835" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +e)^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{3}(\\lambda -1)^{5},$ the nullity of $(D+eI)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(D+eI)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D+eI)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D+eI)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(D+eI)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(D+eI)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D+eI)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(D+eI)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3633 :topic/title "Ranks of Generalized Eigenvectors"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153072} :activity/id #uuid "7574e502-b35e-5436-976a-d2bbc46b222f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153361 :tutorial/content "**Complex vectors** are vectors whose entries are *complex numbers*, such as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n In general, $\\mathbb{C}^n$ is the space of complex vectors with $n$ entries.\n\nComplex vectors work mostly the same as real vectors (we can add them, multiply them by a scalar, etc). However, complex vectors have a few extra properties:\n\n- We can take the complex conjugate of a vector by taking the complex conjugate of each component:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Complex vectors have a real and imaginary part. This means that we can write a vector in the form $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{\\color{blue}a} + \\textrm{i}\\mathbf{\\color{red}b},$ where ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}} = {\\color{blue}\\textrm{Re}(\\mathbf{x})}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{b}} = {\\color{red}\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{x})}.$\n\nThe real part of the vector contains the real part of each entry, and the imaginary part of the vector contains the imaginary part of each entry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a1bb120-504f-5cb4-978c-b6e7712d5665" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4042 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dcbcd939-aa6d-5b42-a367-2330c71dd0d3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b677c92-5390-5cd1-b1d3-b84d180c2b3e"} :step/id #uuid "9a5107fa-fa40-5d8b-a7c9-1c956e2146b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152168 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "689f9f44-4776-56a7-915f-d0dab0755002"} :step/id #uuid "89969538-b3cd-5484-917b-1e9e4c7a6e2b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153201}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152206 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eaed693-68df-5da8-942d-408d6871358a"} :step/id #uuid "519e6eb2-acac-5f16-b760-eec54f701882"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153423 :tutorial/content "The complex conjugate of vectors has a few useful properties:\n\n- We can distribute the complex conjugate over individual vectors in a sum: \n\n$$\n\\overline{\\mathbf{z}_1 + \\mathbf{z}_2} = \\overline{\\mathbf{z}_1} + \\overline{\\mathbf{z}_2}\n$$\n\n- We can distribute the complex conjugate over the scalar and the corresponding vector:\n\n$$\n\\overline{k \\mathbf{z}} = \\overline{k} \\cdot \\overline{\\mathbf{z}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ad9cb744-a97a-521e-b9c8-29ae137721bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6189 :tutorial/title "Properties of Vectors Over the Complex Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "be12b013-a739-53f2-9bc3-8d5526146361" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153139}}] :activity/title "Vectors Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152348}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049192 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151958 :question/id #uuid "203ff7a1-64cf-5564-8e7c-7ff9f652911a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6559" :question/problem "If ![](images/51/518a42d25cd993f4bca2498bd9e91a466c7f0c4d71e109b6ab95c9717d34e9e0.png) find $\\,\\overline{\\mathbf{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the complex conjugate of a vector, we take the complex conjugate of the entries of the vector. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\overset{―}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2+5\\text{i} \\\\ 5-9\\text{i} \\\\ -7+8\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\overset{―}{-2+5\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{5-9\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{-7+8\\text{i}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-5\\text{i} \\\\ 5+9\\text{i} \\\\ -7-8\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b677c92-5390-5cd1-b1d3-b84d180c2b3e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c15019a-14de-5889-9857-e9657f36a91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5+\\text{i} \\\\ -1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325015} {:answer/id #uuid "abe58216-ffed-507a-af3a-76ed551b0aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5+\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325431} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c45b42-fce2-546b-9197-c5d93a38c70f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5+\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325541} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf7ff3e-f2d0-5c04-b754-d3444e295928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1-2\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326189} {:answer/id #uuid "fc36ecb4-59e7-5675-b05b-4c40451664ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5+\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325431} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4335159-f462-5d95-9c17-f1631036c6bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326657}] :question/id #uuid "b0d06576-e31f-5746-8662-1003ad31dfe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-38387" :question/problem "Given the vectors ${x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}+\\text{i}\\cdot {x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the complex conjugate of a vector, we take the complex conjugate of the entries of the vector. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1} & =\\overset{―}{\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\overset{―}{1+\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{2+2\\text{i}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =\\text{i}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i}-4{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\ 2\\text{i}+3{\\text{i}}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+2\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}+\\text{i}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4+2\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5+\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d74d6d-da2c-505f-93dc-ad2d12d7a35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3+2\\text{i} \\\\ -1+\\text{i} \\\\ -5+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324052} {:answer/id #uuid "0fdd7a94-3842-576c-bc0d-3a1a14655f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324117} {:answer/id #uuid "353bb2f2-d36a-5ce1-84e0-93f188ca4daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i} \\\\ 5-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324407} {:answer/id #uuid "64b4f3d9-3423-5249-831c-1ce9bd7c538d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 5+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324805} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba195b-673a-5d03-b271-ab96e31f9024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i} \\\\ 3-5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 5+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324805} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a78790-54e7-5047-8c1e-608576463675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326265}] :question/id #uuid "019e7591-719d-591c-b80d-59dac2747689" :question/math-academy-id "q-38389" :question/problem "If $\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i} \\\\ 5-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ then $\\,\\overset{―}{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the complex conjugate of a vector, we take the complex conjugate of the entries of the vector. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\overset{―}{\\begin{bmatrix}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i} \\\\ 5-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\overset{―}{3+2\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{5-3\\text{i}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 5+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055bd9a7-2f11-5b7c-8c4a-f5efdb74819a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263048} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2f4751-679c-5a22-9bf9-d1ea870b128b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263266} {:answer/id #uuid "28041916-cdad-563f-87c5-91acdc7d6e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263348} {:answer/id #uuid "36d6d8ab-9587-526c-bcb5-9b7c3a804186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263487} {:answer/id #uuid "c62afad8-5ca7-575a-acac-10dd3b8ae05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3-5\\text{i} \\\\ 3-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263348} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b7077d-f3a3-5dd3-8d95-e32f483dbaa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.